Note that this is not the original rep of the paper.
1. The original rep is in matlab, and I move the files into matlab_original.
2. This is the conversion from matlab to python implementation with the help of LLM.
3. Some little changes was made to make sure the positive definite, for example, the post-process of the X_value before feed it into the cholesky. We use MOSEK/CVXOPT instead of SCS because the latter always causes non-positive definite because of inaccuracy. And in the SDP relaxation, we make a tighter constrain by use a minimum eigenvalue constraint: X - min_eig * I >> 0 to make the 'non-positive definite' less frequent.
4. Also changed the code to solve the bug in the original code that the wcr and acr has different length(wcr does not have the value for the last iteration).
5. Need to mention the drawbacks of this algorithm, which is always get non-positive definite result even we set the X >= 0 as constrains. And we have tried many solutions, like post-process to make sure its positive-definite, or use X - min_eig * I >> 0, or other methods.
6. Note that this algorithm always automatically uses many cores/threads, need to constain the cores uses if we want to have fair comparison.
7. We have made two new files: demo.m and WCROpt.m, which increase the robustness by using MOSEK instead of SDPT3, and checks postive-definite and post-process